    "q-109774": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109774",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ for every matrix $A?$\n\nI. $\\text{Col}(A)$\n\nII. $\\text{Null}(A)$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}({A}^{T})$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ is the left null space of $A.$ This consists of vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${A}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$\n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${A}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${A}^{T}$, that is, each column of $A$, by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({A}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the column space of $A,$ which is $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14154359/assets/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
