    "q-109856": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109856",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ for all compatible matrices $A$ and $B?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T})$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(A+B)$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by $A+B,$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by $A+B,$ we actually multiply each row of $A+B$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}(A+B)$ must be orthogonal to the row space of $A+B,$ which is $\\text{Col}((A+B)^{T})=\\text{Col}({A}^{T}+{B}^{T}).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14154359/assets/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
