    "q-109857": {
      "dom_id": "step-q109857",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following subspaces must be orthogonal to $\\text{Null}({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})$ for all compatible matrices $X$ and $Y?$\n\nI. $\\text{Null}(YX)$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})$\n\nIII. $\\text{Col}(YX)$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\text{Null}({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})$ contains all vectors that, when pre-multiplied by ${X}^{T}{Y}^{T},$ result in the zero-vector $0.$ \n\nWhen pre-multiplying a vector by ${X}^{T}{Y}^{T},$ we actually multiply each row of ${X}^{T}{Y}^{T}$ by that vector. So, $\\text{Null}({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})$ must be orthogonal to the row space of ${X}^{T}{Y}^{T},$ which is $\\text{Col}(({X}^{T}{Y}^{T})^{T})=\\text{Col}(YX).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"\n\n**Note:** In general, the four fundamental subspaces of a matrix $A$ can be schematically visualized, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14154359/assets/de22dd5b352e1d5be48f4b6ed3b2a4657ae623c1bd288d3464559758ea334af7.png)",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
